{
 "about": "D-SINK-115, D-SINK-116, D-SINK-117 (2026-11-16, round 5 of the security review): how a source address's authority and host name are read, one file for both mirrors (CLAUDE.md: a rule in two languages gets one shared fixture). `tables` are the shipped tables as both mirrors must hold them: `delimiterFolds` (the code points whose compatibility form holds / ? # @ or :, which CPython's urlsplit refuses in an authority), `invisibleRanges` (Cf, Default_Ignorable_Code_Point and stringprep's B.1), `hostAscii` (the ASCII a host name may hold), and two lists each held to the engine that can compute them: `defaultIgnorable` (node's \\p{Default_Ignorable_Code_Point}, which Python's unicodedata lacks; the python suite builds the invisible table from it) and `b1` (stringprep's B.1, which node lacks; the node suite does the same), and `asciiFolds` (the code points whose NFKD holds ASCII outside hostAscii, each runtime's own fold held to it). `vectors` run through validate_source_url (tests/test_host_grammar.py) and Model.validateSourceUrl (tests/Model.test.js), `why` being read_authority's and Model.readAuthority's reason. `connection` runs through the helper's host_name_refused (the connection layer) only. `stateReaders` are stored sources through both state readers (normalize_state, Model.parseState): the owner's decision of 2026-11-16 drops a source whose address holds a newly refused shape and clears a guide URL holding one, and keeps every other source as stored. `atoms` and `seeds` are the differential's generator classes (tests/test_host_grammar.py): helper and model over the same corpus, with the invariants the URL library itself checks. Every value is hex or an escape: the file is ASCII.",
